---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import CaseStudyBlock from '../../components/CaseStudyBlock.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Improving a B2C Tech Bootcamp Admissions Pipeline | sivani.me"
	description="Discovered issues in information coherency and organization of content through research, designed solutions to resolve these issues, and implemented the changes myself in Webflow."
>
	<SiteHeader />
	<div class="content">
		<main>
			<section class="hero">
				<a class="back-link" href="/">
					<svg viewBox="0 0 17 17" width="17" height="17" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
						<path
							d="M9.1 3.7 L4.3 8.5 L9.1 13.3"
							stroke="currentColor"
							stroke-width="1"
							stroke-linecap="round"
							stroke-linejoin="round"
							fill="none"
						/>
						<path d="M4.3 8.5 H13.7" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" fill="none" />
					</svg>
					<span>Home</span>
				</a>
				<div class="hero__body">
					<div class="hero__heading">
						<h1>Improving a B2C Tech Bootcamp Admissions Pipeline</h1>
						<p class="hero__company">Company: Code Chrysalis</p>
					</div>
					<div class="hero__image">
						<img src="/images/case-study-2/cover.svg" alt="" />
					</div>
					<hr />
					<p>
						Discovered issues in information coherency and organization of content through research, designed
						solutions to resolve these issues, and implemented the changes myself in Webflow.
					</p>
					<hr />
					<p class="hero__skills">
						Skills: heuristic evaluation, competitive analysis, workshop facilitation, stakeholder interviews,
						survey design, usability testing, information architecture, ui design, data synthesis, project
						management
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<SectionHeading text="Summary" />

			<section class="summary">
				<h2>Challenge</h2>
				<p>
					The site had never been evaluated holistically, and was the main entryway into our admissions pipeline
					for our B2C engineering bootcamps. One of the company's main goals during this time was to increase the
					number of students per cohort. The Admissions team had also noticed that prospective students frequently
					asked questions that they would have assumed would be addressed earlier in the pipeline, by the
					information on the site.
				</p>

				<h2>Key insight</h2>
				<p>
					Through data I synthesized from stakeholder interviews, heuristic evaluations, and a competitive
					analysis workshop, I discovered 95 unique issues on the site, 28% of which classified as
					mission-critical i.e. issues that could make-or-break whether a visitor ever makes it to the bootcamp
					application form. If I had to sort the majority of the critical issues under one theme, that theme would
					be "Information Coherency." For example, call-to-action buttons did not clearly signal what action they
					would perform when clicked.
				</p>

				<h2>Outcomes</h2>
				<ul>
					<li>
						I made the CTAs and path to applying more quick and more clear by adding an apply button to the
						navbar - just one click to reach the application form.
					</li>
					<li>
						The application form was embedded directly onto our site, instead of taking users to an external
						third-party site to fill out the form. This reduced friction, and also allowed us to collect crucial
						metrics about how users engaged with the application form that we could not previously track.
					</li>
					<li>
						I made several improvements to the clarity of information on the site: for example, I added
						tax-labels to the prices so the actual monetary cost to customers was transparent and clear.
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					All these changes led to an increase in apply button clicks and submissions of the application form –
					meaning more potential students were entering the pipeline for our bootcamps. The Admissions team also
					reported better feedback from students about the clarity of the site's content, and less "obvious"
					questions about our program.
				</p>
			</section>

			<SectionHeading text="Deep dive" />

			<CaseStudyBlock image="/images/case-study-2/context.svg" title="Context">
				<p>
					After some time on the Product team, Code Chrysalis's external website was handed over to me to manage
					– it had previously been managed by the marketing teams, and updated by contractors.
				</p>
				<p>
					This meant the site had never been evaluated holistically. Updates were historically made without a
					design process in place, and no evaluation of whether an implemented change had actually solved the
					problem that had inspired it in the first place. This lack of end-to-end process was understandable –
					the site was simply one of many responsibilities that fell under the Marketing team.
				</p>
				<p>
					Finally having ownership of the site as a dedicated Product Designer meant I had the opportunity to
					evaluate where it was succeeding, and where it needed improvement. I kept in regular communication with
					the B2C marketing team lead and the Admissions team, who had previously been accountable for the site's
					content and performance.
				</p>
				<p>
					Conducting stakeholder interviews with staff from Admissions and Marketing led me to discover something
					that I realized needed further research: the Admissions team told me they repeatedly had to answer
					questions they felt the site should have already answered clearly – applicants seemed more confused than
					they should have been by the time they had their first chat with Admissions. Since one of our
					company-wide goals was to increase the number of students per bootcamp, it was critical that we improved
					the pipeline from discovery, to application, to admission.
				</p>
			</CaseStudyBlock>

			<CaseStudyBlock image="/images/case-study-2/my-role.svg" title="Your Role">
				<p>
					I was the sole designer on this project, and owned the entire process end-to-end: I conducted research,
					identified problems, designed and implemented solutions, and measured their impact. I managed this
					project as a cross-team collaboration between the Product and B2C teams.
				</p>
			</CaseStudyBlock>

			<CaseStudyBlock image="/images/case-study-2/process.svg" title="Process">
				<p>
					Performed a solo heuristic evaluation using Nielsen's 10 standard usability heuristics – I navigated the
					site and recorded my thoughts, sorted under heuristic categories.
				</p>
				<p>
					Facilitated a cross-team Competitive Analysis Workshop that compared our site with our direct
					competitor's site – 12 participants attended, sorted into teams, each given a different set of user
					prompts (derived from user stories) to evaluate the site.
				</p>
				<p>Conducted stakeholder interviews with staff in admissions and marketing, and sent out custom internal surveys.</p>
				<p>Designed and ran a usability test with 2 internal participants to evaluate information clarity and application flow.</p>
			</CaseStudyBlock>

			<CaseStudyBlock image="/images/case-study-2/key-insights.svg" title="Key Insight">
				<p>
					After I processed the data I'd uncovered through interviews and surveys, I ended up with 95 unique
					issues found – and roughly 28% of those issues were flagged "mission-critical," i.e. issues that could
					make-or-break whether a visitor ever makes it to the bootcamp application form. If I had to sort the
					majority of the critical issues under one theme, that theme would be "Information Coherency."
				</p>
				<p>
					Example 1: multiple users did not understand which call-to-action (CTA) did what, and which one actually
					took you to the application form. Some people thought that the "Book a Consultation" button was a
					necessary step towards applying – even though it wasn't – just because of its positioning close to the
					end of the page.
				</p>
				<p>
					Example 2: a user performing a quick skim mistook the meaning of a crucial number. We had the figure
					¥5.1M advertised on our page as the average salary a bootcamp graduate earned after attending our
					program – but this figure was mistaken to be the tuition of our bootcamp, when our tuition was actually
					priced much lower.
				</p>
			</CaseStudyBlock>

			<CaseStudyBlock image="/images/case-study-2/outcomes.svg" title="Outcome">
				<p>
					To address the various issues related to Information Coherency that I'd discovered, I designed and
					implemented multiple solutions. Design was done in Figma, and development was done in Webflow.
				</p>
				<p><strong>Highlights:</strong></p>
				<ul>
					<li>
						I made the CTAs and path to applying more clear:
						<ul>
							<li>
								Added apply button to navbar, where it was always visible and took a single click to access,
								instead of being buried in a menu requiring multiple clicks
							</li>
							<li>
								Embedded the application form in our website, instead of having it live at an external link. This
								reduced friction, and also allowed us to collect crucial metrics about how users engaged with the
								application form that we could not previously track. This was data we were able to use to track the
								ongoing success of our application form, and make future tweaks to the form as needed.
							</li>
						</ul>
					</li>
					<li>
						I improved the information legibility and trust:
						<ul>
							<li>Added tax-inclusion labels to costs so final cost was clear</li>
							<li>Updated design so that it was clear that the monetary figure being shown was not tuition, but average expected salary</li>
							<li>Automated year in footer so site wouldn't look outdated and neglected</li>
						</ul>
					</li>
				</ul>
				<p>
					While the change wasn't immediate, I eventually saw an increase in apply button clicks and submissions
					of the application form – meaning more potential students were entering the pipeline for our bootcamps.
					The Admissions team also reported better feedback from students about the clarity of the site's
					content, and less "obvious" questions about our program.
				</p>
			</CaseStudyBlock>

			<div class="cta-wrap">
				<a class="cta-button" href="/">&larr; Home</a>
			</div>
		</main>
	</div>
	<SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
	.hero {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		padding: var(--space-padding-large);
		gap: var(--space-padding-medium);
	}

	.back-link {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: var(--space-micro);
		width: fit-content;
		text-decoration: none;
		color: var(--color-dark-gray);
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: var(--text-micro);
		transition: color 0.2s ease;
	}

	.back-link:hover {
		color: var(--color-medium-green);
	}

	.hero__body {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--space-padding-large);
	}

	.hero__heading {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--space-medium);
	}

	.hero__heading h1 {
		margin: 0;
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: var(--text-h1);
		font-weight: 500;
	}

	.hero__company {
		font-size: var(--text-micro);
		color: var(--color-light-grey);
	}

	.hero__image {
		aspect-ratio: 2000 / 1125;
		border: 1px solid var(--color-dark-gray);
		border-radius: 5px;
		overflow: hidden;
	}

	.hero__image img {
		width: 100%;
		height: 100%;
		object-fit: cover;
	}

	.hero__body hr {
		width: 100%;
		height: 1px;
		margin: 0;
		border: none;
		background-color: var(--color-light-grey);
		border-radius: 5px;
	}

	.hero__body p {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: var(--text-body);
		line-height: 1.5;
	}

	.hero__skills {
		font-size: var(--text-micro);
		color: var(--color-light-grey);
	}

	.summary {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--space-padding-medium);
		padding: var(--space-padding-large);
	}

	.summary h2 {
		margin: 0;
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: var(--text-h2);
		font-weight: 500;
	}

	.summary p {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: var(--text-body);
		line-height: 1.5;
	}

	.summary ul {
		margin: 0;
		padding-left: 1.2em;
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: var(--space-medium);
		font-size: var(--text-body);
		line-height: 1.5;
	}

	.cta-wrap {
		padding: 0 var(--space-padding-large) var(--space-padding-large);
	}

	.cta-button {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		height: 33px;
		border: 1px solid var(--color-dark-gray);
		border-radius: 18px;
		background-color: var(--color-light-purple);
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: var(--text-micro);
		text-decoration: none;
		color: var(--color-dark-gray);
		transition: filter 0.2s ease;
	}

	.cta-button:hover {
		filter: brightness(0.93);
	}
</style>
